<interrogationes unitas="css_lexema">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I tokenize a CSS source and walk the tokens?
    <responsum symbolum="css_lexare CssLexema xar_obtinere css_lexema_genus_nomen">css_lexare(piscina,
      fons, mensura) returns a Xar of CssLexema stored BY VALUE, so
      xar_obtinere(lexemata, k) gives a CssLexema* directly. The last
      token is always CSS_LEX_FINIS with an empty valor and offset ==
      mensura, so a loop can stop on the kind instead of counting.
      css_lexema_genus_nomen gives a printable kind name for
      debugging.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I free or reuse the source buffer after css_lexare returns?
    <responsum symbolum="css_lexare CssLexema">No. Nothing is copied:
      every valor points into fons (a view, not NUL-terminated), so
      the buffer must outlive the tokens. Never write through a valor
      either; the cast away from const is only there because chorda
      holds an i8*.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get the original text back from the tokens?
    <responsum symbolum="css_lexemata_emittere css_lexare">css_lexemata_emittere(piscina,
      lexemata) concatenates every valor. Because the lexer is total
      and keeps whitespace and comments as tokens, the result equals
      the input byte for byte for ANY input, including invalid CSS and
      NUL bytes. The tests use it as the fidelity oracle.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the lexer emit whitespace and comment tokens when the CSS spec discards comments?
    <responsum symbolum="css_lexare" via="include/css_lexema.h css/fontes/css_arbor.c">For byte
      fidelity and so tools can query comments. CSS_LEX_SPATIA,
      CSS_LEX_COMMENTARIUM and CSS_LEX_COMMENTARIUM_IMPERFECTUM stay
      in the stream; a consumer that wants spec behaviour filters them
      out. css_arbor_parsare (css/fontes) instead attaches them as
      trivia to the surrounding tokens.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are the IMPERFECTA token kinds?
    <responsum symbolum="css_lexare">Tokens that are cut short or
      malformed.
      CSS_LEX_CHORDA_IMPERFECTA is a string ended by a raw newline
      (the newline is NOT consumed, it starts the next token) or by
      end of input. CSS_LEX_URL_IMPERFECTA is a bad url( or one open
      at EOF. CSS_LEX_COMMENTARIUM_IMPERFECTUM is a comment open at
      EOF. For a string or url left open at EOF the spec returns a
      valid token plus a parse error; this lexer names the problem in
      the token kind instead. Here newline means \n, \r or \f. No
      input makes the lexer fail.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I get the decoded value of an escaped identifier or string?
    <responsum symbolum="css_lexare" via="css/fontes/css_arbor.c">Not from this library: escapes are
      understood only far enough to find token boundaries, and valor
      stays verbatim (\41 stays three bytes, plus the one whitespace
      byte after a hex escape, which belongs to it). The header assigns
      decoding to the tree stage, but css_arbor_parsare
      (css/fontes) keeps valores verbatim too. One consequence: url(
      is recognised by its raw three bytes (any case), so an escaped spelling such as u\72l(
      lexes as CSS_LEX_FUNCTIO, not CSS_LEX_URL.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what units are the linea and columna of a CssLexema in?
    <responsum symbolum="css_lexare CssLexema">Both are 1-based and count
      BYTES, not characters: a multi-byte UTF-8 letter advances
      columna by its byte length. offset is the 0-based byte offset.
      Only \n starts a new line in this count, although the lexer
      itself treats \r and \f as newlines inside strings, so a file
      with bare \r line ends reports everything on line 1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
